{"id":"7af51d57-4adb-4eb5-8024-7d904bef11d7","object":"chat.completion","created":1785008974,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    from collections import deque\\n    nodes = {n['id']: n for n in case['nodes']}\\n    children = {nid: set() for nid in nodes}\\n    for n in case['nodes']:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].add(n['id'])\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    removed = set()\\n    initial = sorted(case['nodes'], key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    queue = deque(n['id'] for n in initial)\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while queue:\\n        node_id = queue.popleft()\\n        if node_id in removed:\\n            continue\\n        visit_order.append(node_id)\\n        node = nodes[node_id]\\n        is_leaf = len(children[node_id]) == 0\\n        if node['removable'] and is_leaf and node['utility'] <= threshold:\\n            removed.add(node_id)\\n            parent_id = node['parent']\\n            if parent_id is not None:\\n                children[parent_id].discard(node_id)\\n                if parent_id not in removed:\\n                    queue.append(parent_id)\\n    retained = [nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed]\\n    retained.sort()\\n    return {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. It's a coding problem where we have a tree of nodes with properties: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Plus a utility_threshold. We need to implement solve(case) that returns retained_node_ids and visit_order.\n\nThe description: \"Maintain and update the syntax-guided reduction work queue across accepted and rejected tree rewrites without revisiting invalidated nodes.\" \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" \"Skip nodes already removed.\" \"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\"\n\nSo it seems like we need to process nodes in order, removing those that are removable, leaves, and utility <= threshold. After removing a node, its parent (if still retained) is added to the queue for reconsideration. The visit_order is the order in which nodes are visited (i.e., popped from queue and processed). The retained_node_ids are the IDs of nodes that remain at the end (never removed).\n\nThe algorithm:\n1. Build a lookup: node by id. Also map parent to children.\n2. Initialize queue: all nodes sorted by depth descending, then stable node-ID order (maybe alphabetical? The IDs are like \"root\",\"n1\",\"n2\",... so we can sort by depth descending, then id string).\n3. Process queue: while queue not empty:\n   - pop node (from front? The queue order: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" And \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" So we need to maintain order. Typically a FIFO queue? But \"queue\" might be a list where we iterate through and also add to end. Or maybe we need to process in order of descending depth + stable ID, and after adding parent, we need to ensure it's processed in correct order later. The spec says \"no_invalidated_revisit\" - means once a node is removed, we don't revisit it. Also \"queue_order\" - the visit_order should reflect the order of processing.\n\n   - If node is already removed (maybe from earlier step), skip.\n   - Record node.id in visit_order.\n   - Check if node is removable and leaf and utility <= threshold.\n   - If yes: remove it (mark as removed). Then, if parent exists and parent is not removed, add parent to the queue (append to end? Or insert appropriately?). The spec says \"enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" So add to queue for later processing. But careful: need to avoid re-processing parent if it has already been processed? The parent may have been processed already (e.g., visited earlier). Adding it again will cause it to be revisited. That's allowed because we need to reconsider the parent after child removal (maybe it becomes a leaf?). So we add to queue.\n\n   - Continue.\n\n   The queue should be processed in order? The initial order is descending depth, stable ID. After appending parent, does it go to the end? That would mean later depths maybe processed again later. That seems plausible.\n\n   But we need to ensure \"no_invalidated_revisit\": we skip nodes that are already removed.\n\n   At the end, retained_node_ids are IDs of nodes not removed. Visit_order is the list of all node IDs in the order they were popped from queue (including root and all nodes that were processed, even if not removed). But note: nodes that are visited but then removed? Their ID still appears in visit_order. The output requires \"retained_node_ids\" and \"visit_order\". So we record all visits.\n\n   Also, the algorithm: \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So if a node is already removed (status), we skip but do we still record it in visit_order? Usually \"visit_order\" indicates the order of processing, but skipping means we didn't actually process it? The problem statement: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" The visit order likely means the order in which nodes were examined (i.e., popped from queue). If a node is removed earlier and its ID is still in queue? That would only happen if we enqueued it later? Actually after removal, we don't enqueue it again because it's removed. But a node could have been enqueued earlier and later removed by another operation? No, removal only occurs when the node is being processed. So when we pop a node, it's either not removed yet. So we will always record it. So we can simply record each popped node in visit_order.\n\n   However, we need to handle case where a node is added to queue multiple times? That's possible: a parent might be added multiple times after each child removal. We need to ensure we skip duplicates? The spec says \"Skip nodes already removed.\" But not about duplicates that are still retained. The queue may contain duplicates. That's fine; we can process it multiple times, but if it's already a leaf and removable? It might be removed on first encounter. If it remains, second encounter would do nothing. But we must avoid infinite loops? Since removal eventually stops. But duplicate entries could cause repeated processing. However, the spec says \"no_invalidated_revisit\" meaning we shouldn't revisit nodes that have been invalidated (removed). Duplicates of retained nodes are okay? Probably fine but might be wasteful. However, we can implement with a set of enqueued nodes to avoid duplicates? The spec doesn't explicitly forbid it, but for correctness we can allow duplicates. But we should ensure we don't get stuck. The process must terminate because each node can be removed at most once, and after removal its parent is reconsidered but the parent may become leaf later. The total number of visits is finite.\n\n   Let's test with given fixtures.\n\n   For fixture example 1:\n   nodes:\n   root depth0 removableF utility1.0\n   n1 depth1 removableF utility0.077\n   n2 depth1 removableT utility0.410 (<= threshold 0.55)\n   n3 depth1 removableF utility0.463\n   n4 depth2 removableT utility0.177 (<=0.55)\n   n5 depth2 removableF utility0.929\n   n6 depth2 removableT utility0.332\n\n   Initial queue sorted descending depth, stable ID:\n   depth2: n4, n5, n6 (IDs sorted: n4,n5,n6)\n   depth1: n1,n2,n3\n   depth0: root\n   So order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root.\n\n   Process:\n   n4: removable leaf? n4's parent is n2. Is n4 a leaf? It has children? No children listed? In data, only these nodes. So n4 is leaf. Leaf check: we need to know if node has children in current tree (excluding removed nodes). Initially all present. n4 utility=0.177 <=0.55, removable=True. So remove n4. Parent n2 becomes enqueued (if not already). n2 is not yet processed, but we add to queue end. Mark n4 removed.\n   visit_order: [n4]\n\n   n5: removable? False -> skip removal. No children? n5 has no listed children, but check if any child still present? not. So leaf but not removable. So no action. visit_order: [n4, n5]\n\n   n6: removable True, utility 0.332 <=0.55, leaf? parent n1. Remove n6, enqueue parent n1. visit_order: [n4,n5,n6]\n\n   n1: popped. Is it removed? No. It is removable? n1 removable=False -> not removable. But at this point, after removal of n6, n1's children: originally n6 was only child? In data, n1's only child was n6. So n1 becomes a leaf. But it's not removable (removable=False). So we do nothing. visit_order: [n4,n5,n6,n1]\n\n   n2: popped. n2 is removable True. It now has children? Originally child n4, but n4 removed, so n2 is leaf now. utility 0.410 <=0.55, so remove n2. Enqueue parent root. visit_order: [n4,n5,n6,n1,n2]\n\n   n3: popped. removable False, has child n5 (still present). Not leaf. No removal. visit_order add n3.\n\n   root: popped. removable False, children: n1,n2,n3? After removals, n2 removed, n1 and n3 retained. root has children, not leaf. No removal. visit_order add root.\n\n   Now queue: after earlier enqueues: we added n2 after n4 removal, then n1 after n6 removal, then root after n2 removal. The queue at the start had n4,n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root. After popping n4, we added n2 to end. Queue becomes: n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root,n2. Then n5 popped, no add. Queue: n6,n1,n2,n3,root,n2. Then n6 popped, add n1, queue: n1,n2,n3,root,n2,n1. Then n1 popped, no add, queue: n2,n3,root,n2,n1. Then n2 popped, add root, queue: n3,root,n2,n1,root. Then n3 popped, no add, queue: root,n2,n1,root. Then root popped, no add, queue: n2,n1,root. Then n2 popped again: but it's already removed. Should we skip? Yes. But is it still in queue? Yes. So we pop it, see it's removed, and probably still record in visit_order? The spec says \"Skip nodes already removed.\" It means we should not process them, but do we still count as a visit? Probably not, because we skip. But we need to decide. \"complete visit order\" likely means the order in which nodes were actually visited (i.e., considered for removal). If we skip because already removed, that's not a meaningful visit. The examples are not given output, so we must infer. Usually in such problems, you record only when you actually examine the node (pop and process). The instruction \"Skip nodes already removed\" suggests we skip them and they are not processed, so they should not appear in visit_order? But the visit_order is supposed to reflect the order of processing. If we skip, we don't process. However, the algorithm description: \"Initialize a queue... skip nodes already removed.\" It implies we should skip those when we pop them, i.e., not perform any action, but we still popped them. The term \"visit\" could mean the act of popping, even if skipped. But the phrase \"without revisiting invalidated nodes\" suggests that we should not even pop them? Possibly we should not add removed nodes to queue again. We have duplicates because we added parent again. But we can check before enqueuing if the node is removed? At enqueue time, parent is not removed, so we add it. After removal, we still have duplicates. To avoid revisiting, we can maintain a set of enqueued nodes to prevent duplicates entirely, or we can check when popping if node is already removed and simply skip and not add to visit_order. The problem says \"no_invalidated_revisit\" - means we shouldn't revisit nodes that have been invalidated. So if we have duplicates, we would revisit them, which is a violation. So we should avoid adding duplicates to the queue. We can maintain a set of nodes already in queue (or visited). But careful: when we remove a child, we want to reconsider the parent. The parent may already have been processed earlier. To properly reconsider, we need to add it again. But if we add it again, we will revisit it, which is allowed because it's not invalidated (unless it was removed). But we must ensure we don't process it twice if it hasn't changed? The spec says \"enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" That implies it's okay to revisit it. So we can add parent again even if it's already been processed. So duplicates are intentional. However, we need to avoid processing the same node multiple times in a way that doesn't change outcome. That's fine. But we must handle skipping if it's already removed. In the example, after n2 is removed, it remains in queue from earlier duplicate. When we pop it after removal, we should skip it. That's allowed. So we should record only visits where the node was not removed (i.e., we actually consider it). Or maybe we should record all pops? I think \"visit_order\" likely includes only nodes that were actually examined (i.e., not skipped). Because the purpose is to show the order of examination. Skipping already-removed nodes is not a real examination.\n\nGiven the lack of explicit outputs, we'll implement as: when popping, if node is removed (status removed), skip without adding to visit_order. Otherwise, add to visit_order and process.\n\nBut we need to be consistent with the fixture. The fixtures have specific answers. We don't have expected outputs, but we must produce something that fits the schema. We'll implement as described.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Represent nodes as dicts; build dict id->node.\n- Build children sets for each parent.\n- Maintain a set of removed_ids.\n- Queue: list, initially sorted by (-depth, id). We can use a list and pop from front (pop(0) is O(n), but fine for small). Or use collections.deque. We'll use deque for efficiency.\n- While queue: node_id = queue.popleft()\n   if node_id in removed_ids: continue\n   visit_order.append(node_id)\n   node = nodes[node_id]\n   # check if removable leaf with utility <= threshold\n   is_leaf = (len(children[node_id]) == 0) # children only existing non-removed? Initially children set includes all child IDs, but when a child is removed, we should remove it from parent's children set to reflect current leaf status. We'll maintain children sets and update when removing.\n   if node['removable'] and is_leaf and node['utility'] <= case['utility_threshold']:\n        removed_ids.add(node_id)\n        parent_id = node['parent']\n        if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed_ids:\n            queue.append(parent_id)\n   # also remove node from its parent's children set\n   # after removal, we need to update parent's children. So we'll do that inside removal condition.\n\nBut careful: when we remove a node, its parent's children set should have that node removed. But we need to maintain children sets dynamically. So we'll have a children dict mapping parent_id to set of child ids. Initially build from all nodes. When we remove, we remove child from parent's set.\n\nAlso note: the children set must reflect the current tree (excluding removed nodes). So when we check is_leaf, we check the current children set.\n\nEdge case: root's parent is None.\n\nProcessing order: The initial queue includes all nodes, but root is at end. After removals, we may add parents. The algorithm should terminate as all nodes eventually processed.\n\nNow, confirm with example after implementing as above:\n\nStart: children: root->{n1,n2,n3}, n1->{n6}, n2->{n4}, n3->{n5}, others empty.\nQueue order: n4,n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root. (We'll pop left)\n\nn4: not removed, visit. leaf? children of n4 = empty -> leaf. removable and utility<=threshold, remove. Add parent n2 to queue (if n2 not removed). children[n2].discard('n4'). Now n2 children becomes empty. visit_order ['n4'].\nQueue now: n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root,n2\n\nn5: not removed, visit. leaf? children empty -> leaf, but removable=False -> no removal. visit_order add n5.\nQueue: n6,n1,n2,n3,root,n2\n\nn6: not removed, visit. leaf, removable True, utility <= -> remove. Add parent n1 to queue. children[n1].discard('n6'), n1 children empty. visit_order add n6.\nQueue: n1,n2,n3,root,n2,n1\n\nn1: not removed, visit. leaf? children empty, but removable False -> no removal. visit_order add n1.\nQueue: n2,n3,root,n2,n1\n\nn2: not removed, visit. leaf? children empty now (n4 removed), removable True, utility <= -> remove. Add parent root to queue. children[root].discard('n2'), so root children become {n1,n3}. visit_order add n2.\nQueue: n3,root,n2,n1,root\n\nn3: not removed, visit. leaf? children n5 still present (n5 not removable, not removed). So not leaf. No removal. visit_order add n3.\nQueue: root,n2,n1,root\n\nroot: not removed, visit. leaf? children set {n1,n3} -> not leaf. No removal. visit_order add root.\nQueue: n2,n1,root\n\nn2: removed (in removed_ids). Skip, do not add to visit_order.\nQueue: n1,root\n\nn1: not removed? n1 is still present. Now visit n1 again: we add to visit_order again. But n1 is leaf (children empty) and removable False, so no removal. So we visit it again. That would cause double visit of n1. Is that intended? According to algorithm, after removing n2, we added root, but root added to queue. Later we pop n1 again because it was enqueued earlier? Actually when we removed n6, we added n1 to queue. That duplicate n1 is still there. So we will process n1 again. But n1 hasn't changed state (it remains non-removable leaf). So we should skip it? The spec says \"Skip nodes already removed.\" It doesn't say skip nodes already processed. But \"no_invalidated_revisit\" suggests we shouldn't revisit nodes that have been invalidated (i.e., removed). For nodes that are still present, revisiting is okay? But it could cause infinite loops if not careful. However, n1 after revisiting adds no change, and it's not removed. So it's a wasted visit but not infinite. But we need to decide whether to allow duplicate visits. The visit_order could contain duplicates. The problem's output schema doesn't forbid duplicates. But typical interpretation: \"complete visit order\" would be the order in which nodes are examined for the first time? Or all times? The algorithm's description: \"Initialize a queue... skip nodes already removed.\" It doesn't say skip nodes already visited. So duplicates are allowed. However, in the example, after we remove n2, we consider root again (added). That is important because root might become leaf after removal of all children? Not here. So root is revisited but doesn't change. That's fine.\n\nBut does the algorithm require that we do not process a node again if it hasn't been invalidated (i.e., if it's already been processed and not changed)? The \"no_invalidated_revisit\" clause says that we should not revisit invalidated nodes, but says nothing about valid revisits. So it's allowed.\n\nThus our algorithm as described will produce visit_order with possibly multiple entries for same node. Let's compute for example:\n\nInitial visits: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root (7)\nThen after queue: n2 (skip), n1 (visit again), root (visit again). So total visits: 9 entries: [n4,n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root,n1,root]\nRetained nodes: any not removed: root, n1, n3, n5? Actually n5 is not removable and not removed. n1 not removed. n3 not removed. root not removed. So retained = [root, n1, n3, n5] (IDs). But we need to check n5: removable False, leaf, utility > threshold? utility=0.929 >0.55, so not removed. So retained = root, n1, n3, n5. Order? The problem just requires array of strings, order not specified but likely alphabetical or the order of some? Not specified. We'll output them sorted or as they appear in original nodes? Probably any order, but we can sort for consistency. I'll output alphabetical order of IDs? The schema doesn't restrict order, so any order is fine. We'll output list of IDs from nodes not removed, maybe in the order they appear in the original list? We'll just collect them as we process? Better to get from nodes dict after all removals: all node IDs minus removed_ids. Order not important. We'll sort for reproducibility.\n\nBut the exact expected output might require specific order based on the algorithm? Since no expected outputs given, we can choose.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nNow, regarding the implementation: we must return a Python function that returns dictionary with keys \"retained_node_ids\" and \"visit_order\". Ensure we use only standard library.\n\nWe'll write the code.\n\nWe also need to handle potential multiple removals leading to empty queue? done.\n\nPotential edge: root may be removable? No, root removable is false.\n\nNow we'll write solution.\n\nAlso note: the nodes list may have \"parent\" as null for root. We'll treat None.\n\nWe'll use deque from collections.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = {n['id']: n for n in case['nodes']}\n    # Build children mapping\n    children = {nid: set() for nid in nodes}\n    for n in case['nodes']:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children[n['parent']].add(n['id'])\n    \n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    removed = set()\n    \n    # Initialize queue sorted by depth descending, then id\n    initial = sorted(case['nodes'], key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    from collections import deque\n    queue = deque(n['id'] for n in initial)\n    \n    visit_order = []\n    \n    while queue:\n        node_id = queue.popleft()\n        if node_id in removed:\n            continue\n        visit_order.append(node_id)\n        node = nodes[node_id]\n        # Determine if leaf (current children set empty)\n        is_leaf = len(children[node_id]) == 0\n        if node['removable'] and is_leaf and node['utility'] <= threshold:\n            # Remove node\n            removed.add(node_id)\n            parent_id = node['parent']\n            if parent_id is not None:\n                # Remove from parent's children\n                children[parent_id].discard(node_id)\n                # Enqueue parent if not removed\n                if parent_id not in removed:\n                    queue.append(parent_id)\n    \n    retained = [nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed]\n    # Sort retained for consistency (optional, but good)\n    retained.sort()\n    return {\"retained_node_ids\": retained, \"visit_order\": visit_order}\n\nTest with example: We'll run mental test.\n\nAs above, visit_order had duplicates. Is that correct? We'll trust.\n\nPotential issue: When we enqueue parent after removal, we add it to queue. But what if the parent is already in queue and later we pop it, we might treat it again. That's fine. But could we remove a parent later and then later again enqueue its parent? That would be correct.\n\nNow, we need to output the Python source as a JSON string. So we'll write the implementation as a string.\n\nMake sure to escape properly. We'll use triple quotes to write source, then embed in JSON. We'll write the entire response as JSON with key \"implementation\" and value = escaped Python source.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON.\n\nBut we must ensure that the solve function signature matches: it takes one argument case and returns dict.\n\nWe'll also handle case where utility is floating point, comparison fine.\n\nNow, we must adhere to explicit case interface: the case argument is exactly the inner payload object. So we can directly access case['nodes'], case['utility_threshold'].\n\nMake sure to not mutate case.\n\nNow produce final response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2018,"completion_tokens":5581,"total_tokens":7599,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1920},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5253},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1920,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":98},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}